! RADIX CONVERSION: CV and LTRCV, and MCP's own time conversion run
! end to end as the oracle.
!
! CONVERT (CV), Reference Manual printed p.73, read at the ink:
!
!   "A field from the accumulator is converted as an integer from
!   binary to decimal or from decimal to binary depending on the
!   setting of the radix modifier.  If the original field is binary,
!   it is obtained at an IMPLIED OFFSET OF 68 and its length is 48
!   NUMERIC BITS.  The decimal result is loaded into the CLEARED
!   ACCUMULATOR AT THE SPECIFIED OFFSET."
!
! LOAD TRANSIT CONVERTED (LTRCV), printed p.73:
!
!   "The addressed STORAGE field is converted ... The TRANSIT
!   REGISTER, STORAGE LOCATION 15, IS CLEARED.  The four low-order
!   bits of the transit register form a sign byte ... The converted
!   field is placed in the transit register POSITIONED TO THE LEFT OF
!   THE FOUR-BIT SIGN BYTE.  THE ACCUMULATOR IS NOT ALTERED BY THIS
!   OPERATION, AND THE OFFSET FIELD OF THE INSTRUCTION IS IGNORED."
!
! >>> BLOCK 1 IS MCP'S PROGRAM, NOT A TEST I INVENTED. <<<
! MCP printed p.24 lines 19-30 convert the 26-bit time clock into six
! BCD digits, and every instruction below is that page's, in that
! order, with only the two indexed scratch references replaced by a
! plain label:
!
!     L(BU,26), $TC, -60        line 19   - GET TIME
!     /I(BU,6), 60, -60         line 21
!     C0011(BU,6), $RM+.54      line 22   -SECONDS IN RM IN BINARY
!     CM0101(BU,6), 0.24($1)    line 23   - SAVE BINARY SECONDS
!     /I(BU,6), 60, -60         line 24   -COMPUTE HOUR AND MINUTES
!     CV(BU), $L, 16            line 25   -CONVERT HOURS TO DECIMAL
!     LTRCV(BU,6), $RM+.54      line 26   - CONVERT MINUTES TO DECIMAL
!     C0011(BU,8), $TR+.52, 8   line 27
!     LTRCV(BU,6), 0.24($1)     line 28   -CONVERT SECONDS
!     C0011(BU,8), $TR+.52      line 29
!     ST(BU,24), XLRIN          line 30
!
! THE OFFSET -60 IS 68 IN SEVEN-BIT TWO'S COMPLEMENT, decoded out of
! that page's own BINARY OUTPUT column, and 68 is CV's implied offset
! and nowhere else.  Line 19 puts the clock there, lines 21 and 24
! each leave their quotient there, and line 25 reads there.  Four
! consecutive instructions agree on one number that the Reference
! Manual fixes independently.
!
! AND LINE 23 IS NOT REDUNDANT, which is the detail that proves the
! model got CV right.  Line 22 puts the binary seconds at offset 0
! and line 23 immediately saves them to memory -- why save what is
! already in the accumulator?  BECAUSE LINE 25's CV LOADS INTO THE
! *CLEARED* ACCUMULATOR.  The seconds do not survive it, and line 28
! reads them back out of memory.  A model that did not clear would
! run this program correctly anyway and still be wrong; a model that
! cleared only the result field would make line 28 convert a value it
! had already converted.
!
! THE CLOCK VALUE IS CHOSEN SO EVERY INTERMEDIATE IS LEGIBLE:
!   45296 seconds = 12:34:56
!   45296 / 60 = 754 remainder 56      seconds = 56
!     754 / 60 =  12 remainder 34      minutes = 34, hours = 12
!   and the six BCD digits are 0x123456 -- which is the answer only
!   if the conversions actually convert.  A model that moved the
!   BINARY values into the same positions would produce 0x0C2238.
!   The two differ in every digit pair, which is the point of picking
!   a time whose parts are all above nine.
!
! BLOCK 2 isolates what block 1 cannot:
!   T5   CV READS AT 68, NOT AT THE INSTRUCTION'S OFFSET.  A decoy
!        value sits at offset 16 where the RESULT goes; if CV read
!        its operand there instead of at 68 the answer would be the
!        decoy's digits.
!   T6   LTRCV DOES NOT ALTER THE ACCUMULATOR -- the manual says so
!        in capitals and nothing else in this file would notice.
!   T7   LTRCV IGNORES ITS OFFSET: the same conversion written with
!        offset 40 lands in the same place in the transit register.
!
! FIVE INJECTIONS, MEASURED, all five built in parallel in private
! copies of bsv/ rather than one compile each:
!
!   CV reading at the INSTRUCTION'S offset instead of the implied 68:
!     T5 = 7 -- THE DECOY -- and the whole MCP chain collapses, T1 = 0
!     and the time reads 0x3456 with the hours gone.  The decoy exists
!     for exactly this line.
!   CV ORing its result in instead of loading into a CLEARED
!     accumulator: T5 = 0x97, which is 0x90 or 0x07 -- the answer with
!     the decoy still underneath it.  Nothing else moves.
!   LTRCV not shifting its digits past the four-bit sign byte:
!     the time reads 0x120305 -- hours correct, because CV put them
!     there, and both LTRCV-fed pairs wrong -- and T7 = 0.
!   LTRCV honouring the offset the manual says it IGNORES: T7 = 0,
!     ALONE.  That one line is why T7 exists.
!   binaryToBcd dividing by sixteen instead of ten, so the digits come
!     out hexadecimal: T1 = 0x0C and the time reads 0xC2E3A.  THIS IS
!     THE FAILURE THE CLOCK VALUE WAS CHOSEN TO EXPOSE -- twelve as
!     0x0C instead of 0x12 -- and it is why every component of
!     12:34:56 is above nine.
!
! EVERY CONSTANT IS A FULL WORD; consecutive DDs pack contiguously.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

! -- BLOCK 1: printed p.24 lines 19-30, verbatim ----------------
START   L(BU,26), CLOCK+.38, -60     ! line 19  the clock, at offset 68
        /I(BU,6), 60, -60            ! line 21  seconds -> RM
        C0011(BU,6), $RM+.54         ! line 22  seconds -> offset 0
        ST(BU,6), SAVSEC+.58, 0      ! line 23  save them: CV clears
        /I(BU,6), 60, -60            ! line 24  minutes -> RM, hours at 68
        CV(BU), $L, 16               ! line 25  hours -> decimal at 16
        ST(BU,8), T1+.56, 16         !          check the hours alone
        LTRCV(BU,6), $RM+.54         ! line 26  minutes -> transit
        C0011(BU,8), $TR+.52, 8      ! line 27  minutes digits -> offset 8
        LTRCV(BU,6), SAVSEC+.58      ! line 28  seconds -> transit
        C0011(BU,8), $TR+.52         ! line 29  seconds digits -> offset 0
        ST(BU,24), RESULT+.40        ! line 30  HHMMSS

! -- BLOCK 2: what block 1 cannot see ---------------------------
        L(BU,8), NINETY+.56, 68      ! 90 at CV's implied offset
        +(BU,8), SEVEN+.56, 16       ! a decoy at the RESULT offset
        CV(BU), $L, 16
        ST(BU,8), T5+.56, 16         ! 0x90 if CV read at 68

        L(BU,8), NINETY+.56, 0       ! accumulator := 90 at offset 0
        LTRCV(BU,8), SEVEN+.56       ! convert 7 into the transit reg
        ST(BU,8), T6+.56, 0          ! accumulator must still be 90

        LTRCV(BU,8), SEVEN+.56, 40   ! the same, with a nonzero offset
        L(BU,8), $TR+.52, 0
        ST(BU,8), T7+.56, 0          ! same digits, offset ignored

PASS    BEW, $

        SLC, (8)2100.0

CLOCK   DD(BU,64,8), 45296           ! 12:34:56, in the low 26 bits
NINETY  DD(BU,64,8), 90
SEVEN   DD(BU,64,8), 7
SAVSEC  DD(BU,64,8), 0
RESULT  DD(BU,64,8), 0
T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T5      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T6      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T7      DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
